35
00:01:40,746 --> 00:01:43,770
likely had one too. That head was packed with

36
00:01:43,770 --> 00:01:46,793
the tools for finding and processing prey. And the

37
00:01:46,793 --> 00:01:49,145
most important tools were in the mouth.

38
00:01:49,495 --> 00:01:53,345
Livyatan's teeth are the reason this animal is famous.

39
00:01:53,345 --> 00:01:56,339
Its nickname, "megatooth whale," comes from them.

40
00:01:56,689 --> 00:02:00,023
The largest teeth are estimated at around 30 centimeters,

41
00:02:00,023 --> 00:02:03,356
about a foot long. These weren't small, flat teeth

42
00:02:03,356 --> 00:02:06,690
built for grinding. They were long, conical, and sharp,

43
00:02:06,690 --> 00:02:10,024
the kind of teeth you'd expect on something that

44
00:02:10,024 --> 00:02:11,135
grabs and holds.

45
00:02:11,485 --> 00:02:14,848
Think about what that means. Most whales today don't

46
00:02:14,848 --> 00:02:18,211
use teeth to catch food at all. They filter

47
00:02:18,211 --> 00:02:20,079
tiny animals through baleen plates.

48
00:02:20,429 --> 00:02:23,956
Livyatan had teeth that could pierce, hold, and possibly

49
00:02:23,956 --> 00:02:27,482
tear. This was a hunter that bit its prey,

50
00:02:27,482 --> 00:02:30,225
not one that strained seawater for snacks.

51
00:02:30,575 --> 00:02:33,743
The first Livyatan fossils were found in the Pisco

52
00:02:33,743 --> 00:02:36,910
Formation of Peru, along the Pacific coast of South

53
00:02:36,910 --> 00:02:37,262
America.

54
00:02:37,612 --> 00:02:41,001
That's an important detail. This wasn't a creature of

55
00:02:41,001 --> 00:02:44,389
one isolated sea. It lived in the warm, productive

56
00:02:44,389 --> 00:02:47,778
waters of the ancient Pacific, where many marine animals

57
00:02:47,778 --> 00:02:48,531
were thriving.

58
00:02:48,881 --> 00:02:52,366
Its neighbors would have included other whales, sharks, and

59
00:02:52,366 --> 00:02:55,850
seabirds. Some of those animals were big. Some were

60
00:02:55,850 --> 00:02:59,335
dangerous. And Livyatan sat near the top of the

61
00:02:59,335 --> 00:02:59,722
menu.

62
00:03:00,072 --> 00:03:03,220
So what did Livyatan eat? This is where we

63
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:06,368
have to be careful. Scientists don't have a video

64
00:03:06,368 --> 00:03:09,515
recording from 13 million years ago, so what we

65
00:03:09,515 --> 00:03:12,663
know comes from fossils and comparisons with living animals.

66
00:03:13,013 --> 00:03:16,338
One line of evidence is bite-like marks found on

67
00:03:16,338 --> 00:03:19,663
the bones of other ancient whales. If those marks

68
00:03:19,663 --> 00:03:22,988
really came from Livyatan, it means the animal was

69
00:03:22,988 --> 00:03:25,944
hunting other large marine mammals, possibly baleen whales.

70
00:03:26,294 --> 00:03:29,281
Could it also have eaten large fish and squid?

71
00:03:29,281 --> 00:03:32,268
Possibly. But the strongest evidence, as far as we

72
00:03:32,268 --> 00:03:35,255
know, points toward other whales. That's the part that

73
00:03:35,255 --> 00:03:38,242
makes Livyatan so unusual. It may have been one

74
00:03:38,242 --> 00:03:41,230
of the few animals in its ocean that could

75
00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:43,221
take on prey its own size.

76
00:03:43,571 --> 00:03:46,627
Livyatan is related to the sperm whale we have

77
00:03:46,627 --> 00:03:49,684
today. But the two aren't identical. Modern sperm whales

78
00:03:49,684 --> 00:03:52,740
have teeth mostly on the lower jaw, and they're

79
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:55,796
famous for diving deep to hunt squid. Livyatan had

80
00:03:55,796 --> 00:03:58,852
large teeth in its upper jaw too, which is

81
00:03:58,852 --> 00:04:00,890
one reason it looked so different.

82
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:05,536
Here's the honest picture. Modern sperm whales are fearsome

83
00:04:05,536 --> 00:04:06,491
deep-sea hunters,

84
00:04:06,841 --> 00:04:09,641
but Livyatan was a different kind of predator. It

85
00:04:09,641 --> 00:04:12,441
may have been more of a surface and mid-water

86
00:04:12,441 --> 00:04:15,240
hunter, going after big prey in an ocean that

87
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:17,107
was warmer and busier than today's.

88
00:04:17,457 --> 00:04:21,129
Livyatan disappeared around five million years ago. No single

89
00:04:21,129 --> 00:04:24,800
cause has been proven. Scientists point to changes in

90
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:28,472
ocean temperature, shifts in prey populations, and changes in

91
00:04:28,472 --> 00:04:31,328
ocean food webs as the likely factors.

92
00:04:31,678 --> 00:04:34,625
What's striking is how much it teaches us about

93
00:04:34,625 --> 00:04:37,572
how fragile giant predators can be. Even the biggest

94
00:04:37,572 --> 00:04:40,520
animals in the ocean depend on the systems around

95
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:40,847
them.

96
00:04:41,197 --> 00:04:44,385
So the next time you hear someone call the

97
00:04:44,385 --> 00:04:47,573
sperm whale the scariest animal in the sea, remember

98
00:04:47,573 --> 00:04:50,762
Livyatan. It was bigger, stranger, and maybe more dangerous,

99
00:04:50,762 --> 00:04:53,950
and it lived in a world we can only

100
00:04:53,950 --> 00:04:55,721
reconstruct from bones and teeth.

101
